WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.623 line:88%
Hello, and welcome to MarkerWise, a tool that asks one simple question.

2
00:00:05.623 --> 00:00:08.247 line:88%
Does the gene decide the drug?

3
00:00:08.267 --> 00:00:11.848 line:88%
Let me show you how it works. It only takes a few minutes.

4
00:00:13.267 --> 00:00:14.738 line:88%
So, here's the idea.

5
00:00:14.738 --> 00:00:19.300 line:88%
A big lab project called GDSC grew nearly a thousand different

6
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.780 line:88%
cancer cell lines, and tested hundreds of drugs on them.

7
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.922 line:88%
A cell line is simply cancer cells taken from

8
00:00:26.922 --> 00:00:30.183 line:88%
a patient's tumour and kept growing in the lab.

9
00:00:30.183 --> 00:00:33.947 line:88%
And each one carries its own set of gene changes.

10
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:36.839 line:88%
That lets us ask a really useful question.

11
00:00:36.839 --> 00:00:39.985 line:88%
If cancer cells have a particular gene change,

12
00:00:39.985 --> 00:00:42.858 line:88%
does a drug work better on them, or worse?

13
00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:50.347 line:88%
Everything starts with these three boxes. A drug, a gene change, and a cancer type.

14
00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:52.214 line:88%
Let's try a fresh question.

15
00:00:52.214 --> 00:00:54.814 line:88%
I'll click the drug box, start typing,

16
00:00:54.814 --> 00:00:58.747 line:88%
and pick lapatinib, which is used for breast cancer.

17
00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:04.089 line:88%
Now the cancer type. You'll notice every option has a green tick or a red cross.

18
00:01:04.089 --> 00:01:09.580 line:88%
Green means there's enough data to give you an answer. Red means there isn't.

19
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.619 line:88%
Breast cancer has a red cross at the moment.

20
00:01:12.619 --> 00:01:16.530 line:88%
That's because the gene box still says BRAF, and only one

21
00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:21.280 line:88%
breast cancer line with that mutation was tested with this drug.

22
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:26.133 line:88%
If I pick it anyway, the tool simply tells me there aren't enough cell lines.

23
00:01:26.133 --> 00:01:27.447 line:88%
It won't guess.

24
00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:28.987 line:88%
So let's fix the gene.

25
00:01:28.987 --> 00:01:34.237 line:88%
Lapatinib targets a protein called HER2, so I'll search for that, and choose

26
00:01:34.237 --> 00:01:39.557 line:88%
the amplification, which just means the cells carry extra copies of the gene.

27
00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:45.314 line:88%
And there's the answer, straight away. The badge gives you the verdict.

28
00:01:45.314 --> 00:01:48.447 line:88%
Here, the gene change predicts sensitivity.

29
00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:52.701 line:88%
In plain English, breast cancer lines with extra HER2 copies

30
00:01:52.701 --> 00:01:58.713 line:88%
needed ninety-two times less lapatinib than the others. That's a big difference.

31
00:01:58.733 --> 00:02:00.544 line:88%
Scroll down a little, though.

32
00:02:00.544 --> 00:02:03.104 line:88%
Under How sure can we be, the tool points

33
00:02:03.104 --> 00:02:05.851 line:88%
out that one group has only four cell lines.

34
00:02:05.851 --> 00:02:09.222 line:88%
So it's promising, but treat it with a bit of caution.

35
00:02:10.433 --> 00:02:15.691 line:88%
For a richer example, let's head back up and click one of the ready-made questions.

36
00:02:15.691 --> 00:02:19.555 line:88%
I'll pick BRAF in melanoma, where we already know the answer.

37
00:02:20.767 --> 00:02:23.420 line:88%
This dot chart is the heart of the tool.

38
00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:28.727 line:88%
Every dot is one cell line, placed by how much drug it took to halve its growth.

39
00:02:28.727 --> 00:02:31.880 line:88%
The further left, the less drug it needed.

40
00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:35.495 line:88%
Orange dots carry the gene change, and grey ones don't.

41
00:02:35.495 --> 00:02:41.347 line:88%
The hollow dots never got down to half growth at all, so their values are estimates.

42
00:02:41.367 --> 00:02:46.043 line:88%
Hover over any dot, and it tells you exactly which cell line you're looking at.

43
00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:51.306 line:88%
Below the chart, How sure can we be runs a few quick checks.

44
00:02:51.306 --> 00:02:54.942 line:88%
The gap is big enough to matter, it's very unlikely to

45
00:02:54.942 --> 00:02:58.880 line:88%
be chance, and a second test of the same drug agrees.

46
00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.121 line:88%
It's also honest about a catch.

47
00:03:01.121 --> 00:03:05.636 line:88%
Another gene, NRAS, is mutated in most of the comparison lines,

48
00:03:05.636 --> 00:03:08.860 line:88%
so that could explain some of the difference.

49
00:03:10.067 --> 00:03:13.228 line:88%
Does it hold in other cancer types puts the

50
00:03:13.228 --> 00:03:16.389 line:88%
same question side by side for each tissue.

51
00:03:16.389 --> 00:03:19.770 line:88%
Here, BRAF-mutant melanoma needed less of this

52
00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:23.813 line:88%
drug than BRAF-mutant lines from any other cancer.

53
00:03:23.833 --> 00:03:27.605 line:88%
And if you want the raw numbers, More detail has everything,

54
00:03:27.605 --> 00:03:31.816 line:88%
including every single cell line, ready to copy into a spreadsheet.

55
00:03:33.033 --> 00:03:38.413 line:88%
Right, now the second tab, Rank drugs. This one tests every drug at once.

56
00:03:38.433 --> 00:03:43.447 line:88%
You pick a gene change and a cancer type, and it runs the same comparison

57
00:03:43.447 --> 00:03:49.047 line:88%
across all two hundred and sixty-five drug screens, then sorts them for you.

58
00:03:49.067 --> 00:03:53.910 line:88%
The drugs that work best on cells with the gene change come out on top.

59
00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:57.594 line:88%
With so many tests, a few will look good just by luck,

60
00:03:57.594 --> 00:04:03.480 line:88%
so a clear link has to pass a tougher test. A possible link might just be chance.

61
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:09.213 line:88%
You can also flip it round, and rank gene changes for a single drug instead.

62
00:04:09.233 --> 00:04:13.865 line:88%
And clicking any row opens it up as a full question, chart and all.

63
00:04:15.067 --> 00:04:20.678 line:88%
Finally, How it works has a copy of this video, explains the method in plain language,

64
00:04:20.678 --> 00:04:25.506 line:88%
and shows the tool getting all eight well-known drug and gene pairs right.

65
00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:28.889 line:88%
One last thing to keep in mind.

66
00:04:28.889 --> 00:04:32.575 line:88%
These are cancer cells grown in a dish, not patients,

67
00:04:32.575 --> 00:04:38.247 line:88%
so think of any result as a lead to test in the lab, not as medical advice.

68
00:04:38.267 --> 00:04:42.575 line:88%
Have a go yourself, using the link on screen. Thanks for watching.

